3.3. Manure storage
Manure is spread on fields as an organic fertiliser to increase crop yield. Up to 90 per cent of nitrogen excreted in manure can be used again, although in reality the rate of reuse achieved is often smaller.209 It is often stored until required. Storage enables manure to be spread both in optimal conditions and in times of low soil nutrients. Storage vessels include concrete, steel or wooden tanks, earth-banked lagoons, pillows and bags. The surface area of manure exposed to the air during storage influences the ammonia loss. This means that lagoons, which have a high surface area, have particularly high levels of ammonia loss, whereas stores designed with increased height and reduced width are more practical for reducing ammonia emissions.210 Fully enclosed storage (e.g. covered stores and bags) has the lowest ammonia emissions.
